## The fine print, but friendlier 🧾

The MIT license above is the part that actually matters legally. Everything
else on this page is decoration.

**You may:**

- Use this material in your own projects
- Modify it until it stops resembling the original
- Show it to your lecturer, your classmates, or a future employer
- Learn from it without asking anyone for permission
- Add better explanations than the ones we wrote

**You may not:**

- Claim you wrote it
- Paste it into a course submission as your own work
- Collect real people's personal data and call it "an activity" 🚨
- Scrape a live website just because the code technically works

**A note about the fictional websites in here** 🕵️

Stacktrace Journal and the other practice pages were written by us for this
class. They are not real publications. Nothing in this repository was copied
from another website.

**A note about scraping** 🕷️

The scraping activities only ever read local files. If you point a scraper at
a real site, that site's terms, its robots.txt, and the law in your country
apply to you. The code is not permission.

**No warranty, in plain English** 🤷

The code is provided as is. It worked on our machines. It is educational
material, not production software, so please do not use it to run a hospital
or a bank. If something breaks, fix it and open a pull request. That is what
the repository is for. 🔧

